    "slug": "operating-principles-behind-every-es-magico-engagement",
    "legacySlug": "the-operating-principles-behind-every-es-magico-engagement",
    "title": "The Operating Principles Behind Every Es Magico Engagement",
    "metaTitle": "The Operating Principles Behind Every Es Magico Engagement - Es Magico",
    "lede": "Almost every vendor has a principles page, and almost none of it can be used. Customer-first. Quality-obsessed. Committed to innovation. You can read a hundred of these and learn nothing, for one simple reason: you cannot catch a vendor violating any of them. A principle that forbids no specific behaviour and costs nothing to hold is decoration that happens to be phrased as a value.",
    "tag": "EsMagico",
    "date": "23 October 2024",
    "author": {
      "name": "Shreyas Sanghvi",
      "role": "CEO",
      "avatar": "av-shreyas"
    },
    "railTitle": "The story",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "sec-1",
        "label": "A principle you can’t be caught breaking isn’t one"
      },
      {
        "id": "sec-2",
        "label": "Falsifiable principles are commercially inconvenient, which is the point"
      }
    ],
    "body": [
      {
        "html": "<p>This is a real problem for a buyer, because what you are actually trying to do when you read a principles page is predict how a partner will behave under pressure two years from now, when something has gone wrong and no one is watching. Stated values written to be safe tell you nothing about that moment. So the operating principles worth publishing are the ones built to be checked, and the test is straightforward: a real principle is falsifiable and it is costly. There is a specific behaviour that would prove we broke it, and honouring it forces us to give something up. If neither is true, it is marketing.</p><h2 id=\"sec-1\">A principle you can’t be caught breaking isn’t one</h2>"
      },
      {
        "image": 0
      },
      {
        "html": "<p>Hold our commitments to that standard, starting with the one the whole company rests on. We operate, and we do not advise and leave. The behaviour that would falsify it is obvious: the moment we hand over a system and leave, the principle is broken and you can see it happen. And it costs us something concrete. We forgo the clean, high-margin exit a consultancy enjoys, and we accept the open-ended liability of running the system in production. The principle is real precisely because breaking it would be visible and keeping it is expensive.</p><p>Next: engineered for scrutiny. The falsifying behaviour is any control that gets retrofitted after an incident, well after the first decision the architecture should have anticipated. If explainability, audit logging, or the human-handover path were added during hardening in place of built into the architecture, the principle was not honoured, and an auditor can usually tell. The cost is speed. Designing governance in from the start makes our first ship slower than a competitor who bolts it on later and demos sooner. We pay that cost on purpose, because the alternative fails at the audit, which is the only test that counts in regulated work.</p><p>Then: outcomes you can measure. This one is falsified the instant we report a metric we did not agree before the work began. Agreeing the number up front and measuring it the same way afterward removes the vendor’s favourite hiding place, which is the comfortable vagueness of a result defined after the fact to look good. The cost is that sometimes the honest number is not the flattering one, and we have to report it anyway. A principle that only holds when the result is good is decoration in the same way.</p><h2 id=\"sec-2\">Falsifiable principles are commercially inconvenient, which is the point</h2><p>Notice what these commitments have in common. Each one rules out a profitable behaviour. The easy handoff. The fast bolt-on. The vanity metric defined after the fact. A principle that costs the vendor nothing is one the vendor will abandon the moment it becomes inconvenient, which is exactly when you need it to hold. The cost is the evidence that the principle is real.</p>"
      },
      {
        "image": 1
      },
      {
        "html": "<p>The same logic applies to sector fluency. The falsifying behaviour is reaching for a generic approach that ignores the binding constraint of the workflow, the adverse-action requirement behind a credit decision or the filing deadline behind a regulatory report. The cost is the efficiency we give up by declining to reuse one-size-fits-all solutions across sectors that are not actually the same. Generic AI is cheaper to deliver. It also breaks on contact with regulated work, so we decline the saving.</p><p>This is why a published principle should make a vendor a little uncomfortable. It hands the buyer a stick. It says, in effect, here is the specific thing you may hold us to, and here is what we have agreed to lose in order to keep our word. A principles page that does not do that is asking for your trust while offering you no way to withdraw it.</p><p>Consider the kind of moment these commitments force. A measured outcome comes in below the version that would have looked best on a slide, and the principle requires reporting the real number to the client’s review committee in place of the convenient one. Or a client initially wants the familiar arrangement where the vendor builds, hands over, and leaves, and the operating principle requires us to decline the clean exit they were comfortable with. These are not hypothetical inconveniences. They are the regular cost of meaning what the page says.</p><p>The honest version is that principles are easy to publish and hard to be bound by, and the difference is entirely in whether they can be checked. Ours are written to be falsifiable and costly because that is the only kind worth standing behind, and because the same discipline runs through everything we ship. Hold us to them the way a regulator holds the running system to account, by asking not what we believe but what we can be caught failing to do. That is the only standard that produces what we promise: outcomes that survive scrutiny.</p>"
      }
    ],
    "images": [
      {
        "src": "/blog/operating-principles-behind-every-es-magico-engagement-1.webp",
        "width": 592,
        "height": 597,
        "alt": "Illustration: A principle you can’t be caught breaking isn’t one"
      },
      {
        "src": "/blog/operating-principles-behind-every-es-magico-engagement-2.webp",
        "width": 690,
        "height": 685,
        "alt": "Illustration: Falsifiable principles are commercially inconvenient, which is the point"
      }
    ],
    "related": {
      "caseStudies": []
    }
